      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(process): report actual UTF-8 byte count in send-keys message",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where the `process` tool `send-keys` action reported JavaScript string length (UTF-16 code units) as the byte count, which is incorrect for non-ASCII literal input.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nEncoded key data is written to process stdin as UTF-8. `string.length` underreports CJK characters and emoji, while `Buffer.byteLength(data, \"utf8\")` matches the bytes sent.\n\n## User Impact\n\nAgents and users now see accurate byte counts in process `send-keys` success messages for non-ASCII literal input.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Exact head: `ea3ede8f91828fa94cf3249b7fecb86f9ebf239e`\n- Fresh-PR AWS Crabbox `cbx_73657fcd5794`: `pnpm test src/agents/bash-tools.process-send-keys.test.ts` — 3 tests passed\n- Focused regression: literal `你好😀` is reported as 10 UTF-8 bytes\n- Fresh-PR live PTY proof on AWS Crabbox `cbx_763095bfe4fa`: real `cat` session returned `Sent 10 bytes to session [redacted].`\n- Exact-head CI repair: pruned one stale max-lines baseline entry already failing current `main`; fresh autoreview clean\n- `git diff --check` — clean\n- Autoreview against `origin/main` — no accepted/actionable findings\n",
